uces to is undecided.  `not_isOperatorMF_of_nontrivial_normal_kazhdan_defect` proves it -- a nontrivial normal property-`(T)` subgroup of the compression defect makes every corona representation kill a nontrivial element, so no injective one exists -- by running `nk_05_assembly` through `normMatrixCoronaUnitaryEquiv`.  Nothing in the module claims the hypothesis is satisfiable; that claim is Question 2 and is not made.  **The deleted explanation is also restored, on the Lean side only.**  The note above records that the sentence saying why *nontrivial* is load-bearing was removed by the sentence-level pass and that `LI.14b`, the row grading it, went with it.  `obstruction_vacuous_at_bot` and `bot_hasKazhdanPropertyT` put the content back where a checker can see it: `⊥` is normal, lies in every defect and has property `(T)` vacuously, and the obstruction at `⊥` reads `bot_le`, which forbids nothing.  The manuscript still does not say this; that is an editorial matter and not a grading one.  Not EXACT for two reasons: the printed clause is a claim about what REMAINS, whose other half is `LI.07` and `LI.08` rather than anything here, and the existence is open by construction.  PENDING FIRST GREEN |
 | LI.16 | p:limits-models | the adjoint spectral estimates require operator-norm multiplicative control, which permutation and Hilbert--Schmidt models do not supply; the soficity of E is proved separately, from its normal form | `LiteralSoficAssembly.markedGroup_isSofic`; `HilbertSchmidtAdjointGap.no_vanishing_modulus_controls_adjoint`; `HilbertSchmidtAdjointGap.exists_hsClose_adjointFar`; `PermutationAdjointGap.no_vanishing_modulus_controls_adjoint_perm`; `PermutationAdjointGap.exists_permClose_adjointFar` | EXACT | EXACT | literal | unconditional | AlekseevThom | YES | **UPGRADE EARNED 2026-08-19: statement stays MISMATCH only because `Sofic/PermutationAdjointGap.lean` is new and has not been through a green build; flip to EXACT on the first green.**  The proof column is held at MISMATCH for the same reason and was EXACT before this edit; it returns to EXACT with the statement column.  The statement MISMATCH was recorded because "no declaration says of the *method* that it requires operator-norm control; what is proved is the counterexample that makes the sentence true".  Half of that was already stale when written and half is closed now.  Stale: `no_vanishing_modulus_controls_adjoint` does speak about the method -- it refutes EVERY vanishing modulus, not a pair of witnesses, and its own docstring says so in those words -- and the row cited only `exists_hsClose_adjointFar`, the witness lemma underneath it.  Closed now: that declaration covers only the Hilbert--Schmidt half, because its witness is a sign diagonal, which is unitary but not a permutation matrix, so it said nothing about the printed sentence’s OTHER model class -- and permutation models are the ones soficity actually supplies.  `Sofic/PermutationAdjointGap.lean` closes it with the same shape of argument at a transposition: conjugation by a permutation permutes matrix entries (`perm_conj_apply`), so a transposition carries e_{i0i0} to e_{i1i1} while its normalized Hilbert--Schmidt distance from 1 is 2/sqrt(d); `no_vanishing_modulus_controls_adjoint_perm` then refutes every vanishing modulus for permutations too.  The dimension-free constant recorded here is 1 rather than the sharp sqrt 2, which is all the refutation needs.  The soficity clause is unchanged and unconditional.  All three printed clauses now have declarations.  a first-green build marker  **EXTENDED 2026-08-19, authored and not compiled** -- the session that wrote it ran no build, so this inherits the `a first-green build marker` above rather than clearing it, and the Lean column is deliberately left as it stands.  `Sofic/AdjointOperatorNormRequirement.lean` supplies the half of *requires* that neither modulus refutation states, the SUFFICIENCY, and states the printed sentence as a single proposition.  `AdjointOperatorNormRequirement.adjointEstimates_require_operatorNormMultiplicativeControl` is a three-fold conjunction: operator-norm control supplies the estimate (`l2_opNorm_conjDouble_sub_le_two`, modulus `2▅W‖`, no dimension loss); no vanishing modulus controls the adjoint defect through the normalized Hilbert--Schmidt distance; and none does through the normalized Hamming distance.  Refuting every modulus says the hypothesis cannot be weakened; PAIRED WITH the positive estimate it says the hypothesis is required, which is what the printed sentence asserts and what the old `MISMATCH` reason was about.  Two further gaps close on the way.  *First*, the note above and `PermutationAdjointGap`'s docstring both say the Hilbert--Schmidt statement about permutations is "equivalently, Hamming"; that equivalence is now a theorem, `hsNormSq_permMatrix_sub_eq`, from the repository's `Hyperlinear.permMatrix_hsDistSq`, and `AdjointOperatorNormRequirement.no_vanishing_modulus_controls_adjoint_hamming` is the refutation stated in the metric permutation models actually come with -- which is also the metric Alekseev--Thom's problem is posed in.  *Second*, the dimension-free constant recorded above as `1`, with `sqrt 2` called sharp, is not sharp: `two_le_l2_opNorm_conjDouble_sub_perm` gets `2`, the same constant as the positive estimate, from the witness `e_{i0i0} - e_{i1i1}`, which a transposition NEGATES rather than merely moves.  So the two sides of the comparison meet exactly and the gap is not an artifact of a lossy constant.  `exists_hammingDefect_and_adjointDefect` is the printed word *multiplicative* on its own.  One bookkeeping fact the new file settles: the corpus carries two permutation-matrix conventions -- `PermutationAdjointGap` writes its own to avoid relying on a library one, while `Hyperlinear` and `Analysis/AmenableQuasidiagonal` use Mathlib's `Equiv.Perm.permMatrix` -- and `permMatrix_eq` proves they agree, which is what lets the Hamming identity reac